# Nothing Is Page-Local

A shared component has exactly **one** definition. Its CSS lives in
`pages/wise.css` or the stylesheet its own module injects; its behavior lives in
that module. A page **hosts** the component — it never restates, ports, or forks
it.

This is permanent and non-negotiable. Every page-local copy silently drifts:
the fork stops matching the shared original, and a change to the real component
lands everywhere except the page that copied it.

## Banned

- Re-declaring a shared class in a page's `<style>` block (`.sc-*`, `.wch-*`,
 `.ws-intent-*`, `.fl-*`, `.chip*`, `.wise-btn*`, and the rest).
- Copying a shared helper into a page `<script>` (measurers, aligners, letter
 splitters, formatters) instead of exporting and calling the shared one.
- A comment that admits the fork — "ported from", "mirrored from", "kept in
 lockstep with", "same as X in js/…" — is the smell, not the fix. Delete the
 copy and use the original.
- Overriding a shared rule from a page to undo it
 (`.sc-belowinput .sc-ask-help { margin-left: 0 }`).

## When a page needs different behavior

The difference belongs in shared code, driven by a measurement, an option, or a
token — not in a page.

```js
// ❌ BAD — page re-measures the composer itself
btn.style.marginLeft = /* 15 lines copied out of wiseai-chat.js */;

// ✅ GOOD — the shared module exports it and every host calls it
import { alignAskHelp } from '../js/wiseai-chat.js';
```

If a surface only wires part of the shared module (e.g. `wireChatComposer`
without `mountWISEcodeAIChat`), fix the shared entry point so that surface
gets the behavior too. Do not patch the gap in the page.

## Before you edit a page's `<style>`

Search the shared sources for the class first. If it already exists there, edit
it there and delete the page's copy.
